@page "/"
@implements IAsyncDisposable
@using Backlog.Infrastructure.FileSystem
@using Backlog.Infrastructure.Sync
@using Backlog.Modules.Inbox.Abstractions.DataTransferObjects
@using Backlog.SharedKernel.Ai
@inject TasksDesktopState State
@inject InboxDesktopState InboxState
@inject IServiceProvider Services
@inject IToastChannel Toasts
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@inject IAppFeatureSettings Features
@inject IAzureFoundryChatClient AzureFoundryChat
@* Every area's answer to "what is your content", collected rather than named:
   the shell picks one by which area is open and never learns what a task, an
   item or a chapter looks like. Empty in a host that composes none, and the
   panel is then simply not offered. *@
@inject IEnumerable<IAiContentSource> AiSources
@inject DevbookScope Devbook
@inject ShellNavigationStore ShellNavigation
@* Injected hard rather than resolved: the Inbox's Capture button is on screen
   whenever the Inbox is, and a host that composes the Inbox without the module
   behind it should fail at startup rather than quietly offer a dead button. *@
@inject ICaptureRunner CaptureRunner

@*
    This page. It owns the header and the AI panel, and decides which surface sits
    below them.

    It used to be called the shell, and it owned the update and feedback windows
    too. Those were never about this page — MainLayout mounts them below every
    route now, in AppFooter — and what is left here is genuinely this screen's:
    the repository scope, the view switch, the side-pane toggles, and the
    surfaces they choose between. The `.shell` class on the wrapper is the older name and stays,
    because it is what every layout rule in app.css is written against.

    There are five, and exactly one of them renders: the workspace — the main view
    the view switch picks, Tasks or Roadmap, with the Inbox and the Devbook beside
    it — the tools surface, the dashboard, the session list and the pull requests
    list. The last four are takeovers — Dev PC Management, the Dashboard and
    Sessions are whole domains, and opening one means the reader has stopped
    looking at the backlog, so it takes the screen and hides every other domain
    concern rather than docking beside them. The roadmap was one of them until it
    became a view. Whichever branch renders owns the page's single <main> landmark.

    The header follows the surface. Every control in it acts on something, and a
    control whose something is off screen is a dead button: the GitHub check
    refreshes the task list, and Ask AI answers from the content of an open area —
    so each of them renders only while what it acts on does. The view switch, the
    side-pane toggles, the takeover groups and the identity region stay, because
    they are true of the screen whatever is on it; during a takeover the view
    switch and the side-pane toggles are the way back, since pressing one closes
    the takeover and shows the workspace.

    What is inside a surface is not the shell's business: Inbox, Backlog
    Management, Devbook, Roadmap, Dev PC Management, Sessions and the
    Dashboard each render themselves from their own project, and the only thing
    that crosses between them here is the repository scope handed to the knowledge
    pane and the dashboard, and the triaged item handed back from the inbox.
*@

<div class="shell shell--wide">
    @* The header is four regions rather than one row of controls: who you are and
       over which repositories, what you are looking at, how the workspace is
       doing, and the things that cut across all of it.

       They used to be one flat strip of near-identical pills, which made a
       multi-select, an exclusive view switch, a panel disclosure and two modal
       utilities look like the same kind of control. Grouping them is the whole
       change — almost every behaviour below is the behaviour that was already
       here. *@
    <header class="app-header">
        @* Region A. It used to open with a "Backlog" wordmark, set smaller than the
           other screens' `.app-header h1` to say it was chrome rather than a page
           title. The window border says the name already, so the wordmark was the
           app telling you twice which app you were in and spending the width of the
           word to do it — the region now opens on the repository scope, which is
           what the identity region was actually for. *@
        <div class="app-header__identity">
            @if (State.Repositories.Count > 0)
            {
                @* One repository on a plain press, one more with Ctrl held — while the
                   task list, the dashboard or the session list is on screen; see ScopeRepository below.
                   The anchor — the first one taken, the one Devbook reads — is marked only
                   once a second chip is pressed: with one chip there is nothing for it
                   to be distinct from, and the markup stays exactly what the single
                   select produced.

                   Fused, because the repositories are siblings read as one control,
                   several of which can be on at once — see Region B for what each
                   shape says. The scope used to be loose chips, which said "related
                   only by kind" of options that are one set. The shared ButtonGroup
                   wears the same header-group shape the view switch does; the chips
                   inside keep their chip
                   classes, because the active fill, the identity edge and the anchor
                   mark all key on those. *@
                <ButtonGroup CssClass="header-group app-header__repository-scope" AriaLabel="Repository scope">
                    @foreach (var repository in State.Repositories)
                    {
                        var selected = State.SelectedRepositoryAliases.Contains(repository.Alias, StringComparer.Ordinal);
                        var anchor = selected && State.SelectedRepositoryAliases.Count > 1
                            && string.Equals(State.AnchorRepositoryAlias, repository.Alias, StringComparison.Ordinal);
                        <ToggleButton BaseClass="chip chip--scope"
                                      CssClass="@RepositoryScopeChipClass(repository.Alias, anchor)"
                                      PressedCssClass="chip--active"
                                      Pressed="selected"
                                      TestId="repository-filter-option"
                                      Title="@RepositoryScopeChipTitle(repository, selected, anchor)"
                                      aria-current="@(anchor ? "true" : null)"
                                      OnClick="args => ScopeRepository(repository.Alias, args)">
                            @repository.Alias
                        </ToggleButton>
                    }
                </ButtonGroup>

                @* Whether the identity hues are drawn at all — here, beside the chips
                   that wear the most visible ones, rather than buried in Settings with
                   the choice of which hue each repository gets. Those are two different
                   questions asked by two different people at two different moments: one
                   is "this project is green", set once and left alone, and this one is
                   "not right now", which is a decision about the screen in front of you
                   and belongs on it.

                   Outside the scope group on purpose. Inside it this would read as a
                   sixth repository, and it is not a scope — using it narrows nothing.

                   A switch rather than a chip, and the difference is what it is about.
                   Every other control in this strip answers "which of these am I looking
                   at" and holds a selection; this one answers "is this layer on", which
                   is the one question a switch is for. So the shared Toggle in its
                   default shape: role="switch" and aria-checked on a real button, with
                   the name written beside the track instead of on it — which is also why
                   there is no aria-label here. Toggle labels itself from that text
                   through aria-labelledby, and a second accessible name would displace
                   the one the reader can actually see.

                   It renders with the chips or not at all. With nothing configured there
                   is no repository to be an identity for, and a control governing the
                   look of an empty set is a control with nothing to do — the same reason
                   the takeover group below hides itself when no takeover is
                   offered. *@
                <Toggle CssClass="header-switch"
                        LabelCssClass="header-switch__label"
                        ControlCssClass="toggle__track header-switch__track"
                        Label="Colors"
                        Id="repository-colours-switch"
                        TestId="repository-colours-toggle"
                        Checked="State.RepositoryColoursVisible"
                        CheckedChanged="ShowRepositoryColours" />
            }
        </div>

        @* Region B — navigation, and nothing else. Left to right: the Inbox
           toggle, the view switch, the Devbook toggle, then the takeovers in two
           groups — the work in progress, and the rest.

           The view switch is the one question every screen answers: which main
           view the workspace shows. Exactly one is always showing, so the switch has
           no "off" and pressing the pressed option changes nothing. The Inbox and
           the Devbook are not views but panes beside whichever view is showing, and
           they flank the switch because they are the workspace's too — the reader
           reads them beside the list or the roadmap, not instead of it. The Tasks
           pane toggle that used to sit between them is gone with the "side panes
           only" state it allowed: the task list is the Tasks view now.

           The shapes. A fused group is a set of sibling destinations read as one
           control — the view switch, and the work in progress (In progress,
           Sessions and Pull requests) — and the pressed
           state says which is on. A standalone option is one on-or-off of its own:
           the two side panes. The loose group is the remaining takeovers, related
           to each other only by being takeovers.

           The view options and the side-pane toggles are also the way back from a
           takeover: pressing one closes it and shows the workspace. During a
           takeover none of them reads pressed, because nothing of the workspace is
           on screen; the view and the open panes are kept underneath and come
           back as they were. *@
        <nav class="app-header__nav" aria-label="Workspace views">
            <div class="app-header__workspace" data-testid="workspace-navigation">
                @if (InboxPaneOptionVisible)
                {
                    <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option header-group__option--loose"
                                  PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                  Pressed="PaneOptionPressed(GlobalPane.Inbox)"
                                  id="inbox-pane-option"
                                  TestId="inbox-pane-option"
                                  aria-controls="inbox-pane"
                                  Title="@PaneOptionTitle(GlobalPane.Inbox, "Inbox")"
                                  OnClick="() => PressPane(GlobalPane.Inbox)">
                        Inbox
                        <FeatureStatusBadge Status="@FeatureStatusSlug(AppFeatures.InboxPane)"
                                            Title="@FeatureStatusTitle(AppFeatures.InboxPane)"
                                            TestId="inbox-feature-status" />
                    </ToggleButton>
                }

                @* The view switch. Tasks, Board and Calendar are always offered; the
                   Roadmap while its flag is on. Tasks' option is still the way back
                   from a takeover. A later step adds In progress here. *@
                <ButtonGroup CssClass="header-group header-group--views"
                             AriaLabel="View"
                             TestId="view-switch">
                    <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option"
                                  PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                  Pressed="ViewOptionPressed(ShellView.Tasks)"
                                  id="tasks-view-option"
                                  TestId="tasks-view-option"
                                  aria-controls="backlog-pane"
                                  OnClick="() => ShowView(ShellView.Tasks)">
                        Tasks
                    </ToggleButton>

                    @* The Board: the Tasks pane's filtered rows in columns of cards,
                       right after Tasks because it is the same rows read another way. *@
                    <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option"
                                  PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                  Pressed="ViewOptionPressed(ShellView.Board)"
                                  id="board-view-option"
                                  TestId="board-view-option"
                                  aria-controls="backlog-pane"
                                  OnClick="() => ShowView(ShellView.Board)">
                        Board
                    </ToggleButton>

                    @* The Calendar: the Tasks pane's filtered rows on a month by due
                       date. Board sits between it and Tasks. *@
                    <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option"
                                  PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                  Pressed="ViewOptionPressed(ShellView.Calendar)"
                                  id="calendar-view-option"
                                  TestId="calendar-view-option"
                                  aria-controls="backlog-pane"
                                  OnClick="() => ShowView(ShellView.Calendar)">
                        Calendar
                    </ToggleButton>

                    @if (RoadmapViewOffered)
                    {
                        <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option"
                                      PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                      Pressed="ViewOptionPressed(ShellView.Roadmap)"
                                      id="roadmap-view-option"
                                      TestId="roadmap-view-option"
                                      aria-controls="roadmap-view"
                                      OnClick="() => ShowView(ShellView.Roadmap)">
                            Roadmap
                            <FeatureStatusBadge Status="@FeatureStatusSlug(RoadmapFeatures.Roadmap)"
                                                Title="@FeatureStatusTitle(RoadmapFeatures.Roadmap)"
                                                TestId="roadmap-feature-status" />
                        </ToggleButton>
                    }
                </ButtonGroup>

                @if (DevbookPaneOptionVisible)
                {
                    <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option header-group__option--loose"
                                  PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                  Pressed="PaneOptionPressed(GlobalPane.Devbook)"
                                  id="devbook-pane-option"
                                  TestId="devbook-pane-option"
                                  aria-controls="repository-devbook-pane"
                                  Title="@PaneOptionTitle(GlobalPane.Devbook, "Devbook")"
                                  OnClick="() => PressPane(GlobalPane.Devbook)">
                        Devbook
                        <FeatureStatusBadge Status="@FeatureStatusSlug(DevbookFeatures.RepositoryDevbook)"
                                            Title="@FeatureStatusTitle(DevbookFeatures.RepositoryDevbook)"
                                            TestId="devbook-feature-status" />
                    </ToggleButton>
                }
            </div>

            @* The work in progress: what the assistants are running and what they
               shipped. Two takeovers of the Sessions context, each under its own
               flag, fused because they are one context's two lists, led by the In
               progress view that summarises both. Each list is a takeover, so
               pressing the pressed one, Escape, or the surface's own close button
               returns to the workspace. Renders only while one of them is offered. *@
            @if (SessionsOffered || PullRequestsOffered)
            {
                <ButtonGroup CssClass="header-group header-group--work"
                             AriaLabel="Work in progress"
                             TestId="work-in-progress-switcher">
                    @* A main view, not a takeover, though it sits with two: pressing
                       it shows the view as a view-switch option does, the Inbox and
                       the Devbook stay beside it, and nothing closes it. First,
                       because it is the summary the other two are the lists behind. *@
                    <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option"
                                  PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                  Pressed="ViewOptionPressed(ShellView.InProgress)"
                                  id="in-progress-view-option"
                                  TestId="in-progress-view-option"
                                  aria-controls="in-progress-view"
                                  OnClick="() => ShowView(ShellView.InProgress)">
                        In progress
                    </ToggleButton>

                    @if (SessionsOffered)
                    {
                        <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option"
                                      PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                      Pressed="SessionsVisible"
                                      id="sessions-toggle-button"
                                      TestId="sessions-toggle-button"
                                      aria-controls="sessions-surface"
                                      PressedChanged="ToggleSessions">
                            Sessions
                            <FeatureStatusBadge Status="@FeatureStatusSlug(SessionFeatures.Sessions)"
                                                Title="@FeatureStatusTitle(SessionFeatures.Sessions)"
                                                TestId="sessions-feature-status" />
                        </ToggleButton>
                    }

                    @* Its own option and its own flag, since it asks GitHub on every
                       opening and the session list asks nothing. *@
                    @if (PullRequestsOffered)
                    {
                        <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option"
                                      PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                      Pressed="PullRequestsVisible"
                                      id="pull-requests-toggle-button"
                                      TestId="pull-requests-toggle-button"
                                      aria-controls="pull-requests-surface"
                                      PressedChanged="TogglePullRequests">
                            Pull requests
                            <FeatureStatusBadge Status="@FeatureStatusSlug(SessionFeatures.PullRequests)"
                                                Title="@FeatureStatusTitle(SessionFeatures.PullRequests)"
                                                TestId="pull-requests-feature-status" />
                        </ToggleButton>
                    }
                </ButtonGroup>
            }

            @* The remaining takeovers, loose: the Dashboard and Dev PC Management's
               Tools, related only by each taking the screen. WorkspaceSurface is a
               single enum because a takeover cannot coexist with the workspace or
               with another takeover, and a group of pressed states describes that
               exclusivity where independent aria-expanded buttons did not. Ask AI
               keeps aria-expanded further down, because that one really is a
               disclosure. Renders only while one of them is offered. *@
            @if (SurfaceSwitcherVisible)
            {
                <ButtonGroup CssClass="header-group header-group--loose header-group--surface"
                             AriaLabel="Surface"
                             TestId="workspace-surface-switcher">
                    @if (DashboardOffered)
                    {
                        <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option header-group__option--loose"
                                      PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                      Pressed="DashboardVisible"
                                      id="dashboard-toggle-button"
                                      TestId="dashboard-toggle-button"
                                      aria-controls="dashboard-surface"
                                      PressedChanged="ToggleDashboard">
                            Dashboard
                            <FeatureStatusBadge Status="@FeatureStatusSlug(DashboardFeatures.Dashboard)"
                                                Title="@FeatureStatusTitle(DashboardFeatures.Dashboard)"
                                                TestId="dashboard-feature-status" />
                        </ToggleButton>
                    }

                    @if (IsFeatureEnabled(DevPcFeatures.SystemTools))
                    {
                        <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option header-group__option--loose"
                                      PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                      Pressed="ToolsVisible"
                                      id="tools-toggle-button"
                                      TestId="tools-toggle-button"
                                      aria-controls="tools-pane"
                                      PressedChanged="ToggleTools">
                            Tools
                            <FeatureStatusBadge Status="@FeatureStatusSlug(DevPcFeatures.SystemTools)"
                                                Title="@FeatureStatusTitle(DevPcFeatures.SystemTools)"
                                                TestId="tools-feature-status" />
                        </ToggleButton>
                    }
                </ButtonGroup>
            }
        </nav>

        @* Region C — how the workspace is doing. Ambient rather than actionable,
           which is why it is the quietest thing in the row and why it no longer
           sits in the middle of the navigation. The auto margin lives here now, so
           the status and the utilities travel to the right edge together.

           The save state used to lead this region and no longer does. It was the
           one thing here that was true of the window rather than of this screen,
           and it was therefore missing from every other route — so it went to the
           band AppFooter mounts under all of them. The sync control stayed, because
           checking GitHub is a thing you do to the list in front of you. *@
        <div class="app-header__status">
            @* Monitoring is a deliberate act, not a background poll: the backlog has
               to open instantly and offline, so nothing here waits on GitHub until
               somebody asks it to. This re-reads everything; the only read made
               unasked is CheckPullRequestsAfterLoad's, which asks about pull
               requests not yet landed once the list is already showing, and blocks
               nothing when GitHub cannot be reached.

               Only while the task list is on screen — the list is what a check
               refreshes, and the dashboard fetches from GitHub on its own. *@
            @if (TasksListOnScreen && IsFeatureEnabled(TasksFeatures.GitHubIntegration) && State.GitHubConfigured && State.HasLinkedRows)
            {
                <IconButton BaseClass="icon-link"
                            id="github-sync-button"
                            TestId="github-sync-button"
                            Disabled="@State.GitHubSyncing"
                            AriaLabel="Check GitHub for issue and pull request updates"
                            OnClick="() => State.SyncGitHubAsync()">
                    @(State.GitHubSyncing ? "…" : "⟳")
                </IconButton>
            }
        </div>

        @* Region D — the cross-cutting concerns, at the far edge and last in
           reading order: not one of them is about the work on screen.

           Ask AI is the one accented control in the whole header. It is a designed
           first-class surface rather than a utility, and holding the accent back to
           a single control is what lets it mean something instead of becoming the
           header's wallpaper — which is what the old all-gold row had made it. *@
        <div class="app-header__utilities">
            @* Offered while there is content to ask about — while at least one area
               with an Ask AI source behind it is on screen. The panel answers from that
               area's own content, not from what the screen is showing of it, so a
               takeover no longer hides the button: the Dashboard and Tools are areas
               too. What does hide it is a screen with no area at all, which is the one
               case where the button would open nothing about this screen. *@
            @if (AiAssistantOffered)
            {
                @* A disclosure, not a pressed state: this opens a panel beside the
                   content, so it carries aria-expanded and stays AppButton rather
                   than ToggleButton. The takeover group above is the other case —
                   a selection, not an expansion.

                   The state is written out rather than bound to the bool: Blazor
                   renders a true bool attribute as `aria-expanded=""` and omits it
                   entirely when false, and neither is a value aria-expanded accepts.
                   Now that this is the header's only disclosure, it is the one place
                   that has to say "true" or "false" — which is what the two takeover
                   buttons did before they became a selection. *@
                <AppButton BaseClass="header-accent"
                           Variant="ButtonVariant.None"
                           id="ai-toggle-button"
                           TestId="ai-toggle-button"
                           aria-controls="ai-assistant-panel"
                           aria-expanded="@(_aiExpanded ? "true" : "false")"
                           OnClick="ToggleAiPanel">
                    Ask AI
                    <FeatureStatusBadge Status="@FeatureStatusSlug(AppFeatures.AiAssistant)"
                                        Title="@FeatureStatusTitle(AppFeatures.AiAssistant)"
                                        TestId="ai-feature-status" />
                </AppButton>
            }

            @* Two utilities left, and they are the two that are still about this
               screen: Ask AI opens a panel beside its content, and settings is
               where this screen's own configuration lives. The version control
               and the Report issue button were neither, and they are now in the
               app-shell footer MainLayout mounts below every route. *@
            <a class="icon-link" href="settings" id="settings-link" data-testid="settings-link" title="Settings" aria-label="Settings">⚙</a>
        </div>
    </header>

    @* Gated the way its button is: the panel follows the areas it reads from, and
       comes back open if the reader left it open, because _aiExpanded is not
       touched by a takeover. *@
    @if (AiAssistantOffered && _aiExpanded)
    {
        <section class="ai-panel" id="ai-assistant-panel" data-testid="ai-assistant-panel" aria-labelledby="ai-assistant-title">
            @* The library's section header. This one stays a div: the panel is
               already a section named by its heading, and the header it had was
               never a landmark of its own. *@
            <SectionHeader Element="div"
                           BaseClass="ai-panel__header"
                           TextCssClass=""
                           EyebrowCssClass="ai-panel__eyebrow"
                           TitleCssClass=""
                           ActionsCssClass=""
                           Eyebrow="Azure Foundry"
                           TitleId="ai-assistant-title"
                           Title="Ask about this content">
                <Actions>
                    <a class="ai-panel__settings" href="settings">Settings</a>
                </Actions>
            </SectionHeader>
            @* Which area answers. With several on screen the reader chooses, on a
               row of pressed states — the same shared group and toggle the header's
               scope chips are — and the last area they opened is pressed by default,
               because it is the one they were just looking at. With one area there is
               nothing to choose, so its name goes into the sentence instead: a group
               of one is a control with no choice in it. *@
            @if (OpenAiAreas.Count > 1)
            {
                <ButtonGroup CssClass="ai-panel__scope" AriaLabel="Ask about" TestId="ai-scope">
                    @foreach (var area in OpenAiAreas)
                    {
                        <ToggleButton BaseClass="chip"
                                      PressedCssClass="chip--active"
                                      Pressed="@string.Equals(area.AreaKey, EffectiveAiScope, StringComparison.Ordinal)"
                                      TestId="@($"ai-scope-{area.AreaKey}")"
                                      OnClick="() => ChooseAiScope(area.AreaKey)">
                            @area.AreaTitle
                        </ToggleButton>
                    }
                </ButtonGroup>
            }
            <p class="ai-panel__body">
                @AiPanelBody
            </p>
            @* The box keeps its own class and its own border, so the library's
               grow wrapper is emitted without one. *@
            <TextArea Bare="true"
                      AutoGrow="false"
                      GrowCssClass=""
                      InputCssClass="ai-panel__question"
                      AriaLabel="Ask about this content"
                      data-testid="ai-question-input"
                      Rows="3"
                      Placeholder="What should I focus on next?"
                      Value="@_aiQuestion"
                      ValueChanged="value => _aiQuestion = value"
                      @onkeydown="OnAiQuestionKeyDownAsync" />
            <div class="ai-panel__actions">
                <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Secondary"
                           data-testid="ai-ask-button"
                           Disabled="@_aiBusy"
                           OnClick="AskAiAsync">
                    @(_aiBusy ? "Asking..." : "Ask")
                </AppButton>
            </div>
            @* The failure used to be a line under this box. It is a toast now: the
               panel is a takeover the reader opens over their work, and a refusal
               that stayed pinned inside it was still there next time they opened it.
               See AskAiAsync, where each catch publishes on its way out. *@
            @if (_aiAnswer is not null)
            {
                <article class="ai-panel__answer" data-testid="ai-answer">
                    <h3>Answer</h3>
                    <p>@_aiAnswer</p>
                </article>
            }
        </section>
    }

    @* Exactly one of these renders, and each one's root is the page's only
       <main>. A takeover is deliberately not a Modal: there is no scrim and no
       focus trap, because the header behind it is not inert — it is where the
       control that closes the surface lives. Modal.razor still owns the modal
       semantics for the update and feedback dialogs, which really are modal. *@
    @if (ToolsVisible)
    {
        <main class="workspace-surface"
              data-testid="tools-surface"
              tabindex="-1"
              @ref="_surfaceElement"
              @onkeydown="OnSurfaceKeyDown"
              data-escape-closes
              aria-label="System tools">
            <ToolsPane OnClose="CloseSurface" />
        </main>
    }
    else if (DashboardVisible)
    {
        <main class="workspace-surface"
              id="dashboard-surface"
              data-testid="dashboard-surface"
              tabindex="-1"
              @ref="_surfaceElement"
              @onkeydown="OnSurfaceKeyDown"
              data-escape-closes
              aria-label="Dashboard">
            <DashboardPane OnClose="CloseSurface"
                           RepositoryAliases="@State.SelectedRepositoryAliases"
                           RepositoryColour="@RepositoryColourFor" />
        </main>
    }
    else if (SessionsVisible)
    {
        <main class="workspace-surface"
              id="sessions-surface"
              data-testid="sessions-surface"
              tabindex="-1"
              @ref="_surfaceElement"
              @onkeydown="OnSurfaceKeyDown"
              data-escape-closes
              aria-label="Sessions">
            <SessionsPane OnClose="CloseSurface"
                          RepositoryScope="@State.SelectedRepositoryAliases"
                          RepositoryAlias="@RepositoryAliasFor"
                          RepositoryColour="@RepositoryColourFor"
                          OnOpenTask="OpenPlanEntryAsync"
                          SessionTask="SessionTaskFor"
                          FocusSessionId="@_focusSessionId" />
        </main>
    }
    else if (PullRequestsVisible)
    {
        <main class="workspace-surface"
              id="pull-requests-surface"
              data-testid="pull-requests-surface"
              tabindex="-1"
              @ref="_surfaceElement"
              @onkeydown="OnSurfaceKeyDown"
              data-escape-closes
              aria-label="Pull requests">
            <PullRequestsPane OnClose="CloseSurface"
                              RepositoryScope="@State.SelectedRepositoryAliases"
                              RepositoryAlias="@RepositoryAliasFor"
                              RepositoryColour="@RepositoryColourFor"
                              PullRequestTask="PullRequestTaskFor"
                              OnOpenTask="OpenPlanEntryAsync"
                              OnOpenSession="OpenSessionAsync"
                              FocusPullRequest="@_focusPullRequest" />
        </main>
    }
    else
    {
        <main class="workspace" id="workspace" data-testid="workspace">
            @* The pane row: the Inbox, the main view, and the Devbook beside it.
               The class, the width variable and the test id are unchanged from when
               it was the <main> itself, because the resizer's JavaScript finds the
               layout by that test id. *@
            <div class="devbook-layout @(DevbookPaneVisible ? "devbook-layout--side-open" : "devbook-layout--side-closed") @(InboxPaneVisible ? "devbook-layout--inbox-before-backlog" : string.Empty)" style="--devbook-panel-width: @_devbookPanelWidthRem.ToString(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)rem" data-testid="devbook-layout">
                @if (InboxPaneVisible)
                {
                    @InboxPaneContent
                }

                @if (RoadmapViewVisible)
                {
                    @* No Escape-to-close here, unlike the takeovers: the roadmap is a
                       view, which the switch replaces and nothing closes — and Escape
                       already means something on it: it puts a grabbed bar back and
                       dismisses the planning dialogs. The roadmap carries its own
                       Planning heading row and no Tasks filter bar; the filter bar is
                       the Tasks view's. *@
                    <div class="roadmap-view" id="roadmap-view" data-testid="roadmap-view">
                        <RoadmapBand />
                    </div>
                }
                else if (InProgressViewVisible)
                {
                    @* A view like the roadmap: no Escape, no filter bar. *@
                    <div class="in-progress-view" id="in-progress-view" data-testid="in-progress-view">
                        <InProgressView Reader="_work"
                                        OnOpenTask="OpenEntryAsync"
                                        OnOpenSession="OpenSessionAsync"
                                        OnOpenPullRequest="OpenPullRequestAsync"
                                        ShowSessions="SessionsOffered"
                                        ShowPullRequests="PullRequestsOffered" />
                    </div>
                }
                else
                {
                    <TasksPane OnOpenSession="OpenSessionAsync" OnOpenDevbookReference="OpenDevbookReferenceAsync" Layout="@TasksPaneLayout" BoardGrouping="@BoardGrouping" BoardGroupingChanged="OnBoardGroupingChanged" LinkedWork="LinkedWorkFor" />
                }

                @if (DevbookPaneVisible)
                {
                    @* The pane edge is the handle. Dragging it is the obvious gesture, so
                       the slider that used to sit inside the pane is gone; arrow keys on
                       the same separator carry the keyboard equivalent. *@
                    <div class="pane-resizer"
                         data-testid="side-pane-resizer"
                         data-pane-resizer="true"
                         role="separator"
                         aria-orientation="vertical"
                         aria-label="Side pane width"
                         aria-valuemin="@MinSidePaneWidthRem"
                         aria-valuemax="@_maxSidePaneWidthRem.ToString(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)"
                         aria-valuenow="@_devbookPanelWidthRem.ToString(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)"
                         tabindex="0"
                         @onkeydown="OnResizerKeyDown"
                         @onkeydown:preventDefault="true">
                        <span class="pane-resizer__grip" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    </div>

                    <aside @key=@("side-pane-stack") class="side-pane-stack" data-testid="side-pane-stack" aria-label="Side panes">
                        @* The anchor and not the whole scope: the pane reads one
                           repository's folders, and the first one taken is the one
                           the reader was already reading beside — unless an entry's
                           reference asked for another; see DevbookRepositoryAlias. *@
                        <DevbookPane RepositoryAlias="@DevbookRepositoryAlias"
                                     OpenReference="@_devbookOpenRequest"
                                     OnOpenReferenceFollowed="OnDevbookReferenceFollowed" />
                    </aside>
                }
            </div>
        </main>
    }
</div>

@code {
    private WorkspaceSurface _surface = WorkspaceSurface.Workspace;

    /// <summary>The main view the workspace shows. Kept while a takeover is up, so
    /// closing the takeover shows the view the reader left.</summary>
    private ShellView _view = ShellView.Tasks;
    private bool _aiExpanded;
    private GlobalPaneSelection _globalPanes = new();
    private double _devbookPanelWidthRem = SidePaneWidth.DefaultRem;
    private double _maxSidePaneWidthRem = SidePaneWidth.MaxRem;
    private DotNetObjectReference<Home>? _selfReference;
    private ElementReference _surfaceElement;
    private bool _focusSurfacePending;
    private bool _workspaceWasHidden;

    private const double MinSidePaneWidthRem = SidePaneWidth.MinRem;
    private string _aiQuestion = string.Empty;
    private string? _aiAnswer;
    private bool _aiBusy;

    /// <summary>The area the reader chose to ask about, by key, or null before
    /// any choice. Session-only and unpersisted on purpose: it is "the one I was
    /// just looking at", which a relaunch cannot know. Every gesture that opens
    /// an area writes it — see <see cref="RememberAiScope"/> — so the default is
    /// the last-opened area, and a pressed chip overrides that until the next
    /// open. Resolved through <see cref="EffectiveAiScope"/>, which falls back to
    /// the first open area when this one has since been closed.</summary>
    private string? _aiScope;

    /// <summary>The sources by area key, taken once: the collection is what a
    /// host composed and does not change while the page is up.</summary>
    private Dictionary<string, IAiContentSource> _aiSources = new(StringComparer.Ordinal);

    /// <summary>The id the refusal carried while it was an inline alert under the
    /// question box. It rides on the toast now, so a selector written against
    /// either surface still finds the sentence.</summary>
    private const string AiErrorTestId = "ai-error";

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        // Reopen on the surface the reader was last looking at rather than
        // always defaulting to the workspace panes. An unrecognised or absent
        // value — first launch included — leaves the field defaults in place.
        if (Enum.TryParse<WorkspaceSurface>(ShellNavigation.LastSurface, out var restoredSurface)
            && Enum.IsDefined(restoredSurface))
        {
            _surface = restoredSurface;
        }

        // The view the workspace was showing. The store already reads a file from
        // when the roadmap was a takeover as the Roadmap view over the workspace;
        // the surface is checked again here so that no path can put the old
        // takeover back on screen.
        if (Enum.TryParse<ShellView>(ShellNavigation.LastView, out var restoredView)
            && Enum.IsDefined(restoredView)
            && char.IsLetter(ShellNavigation.LastView[0]))
        {
            _view = restoredView;
        }

        if (_surface == WorkspaceSurface.Roadmap)
        {
            _surface = WorkspaceSurface.Workspace;
            _view = ShellView.Roadmap;
        }

        // Same restore for which side panes were open — "Tasks" among them, from
        // a file written before the view switch, names no pane and is skipped. This
        // is what makes it survive
        // Settings and back, not only a full relaunch: the Router disposes and
        // reconstructs this component on every route change, so a fresh
        // GlobalPaneSelection is exactly what a bare `new()` field initializer
        // would otherwise hand back.
        var restoredPanes = ShellNavigation.LastEnabledPanes
            .Select(name => GlobalPaneSelection.TryParsePersistedPane(name, out var pane) ? pane : (GlobalPane?)null)
            .Where(pane => pane.HasValue)
            .Select(pane => pane!.Value)
            .ToArray();

        _globalPanes = new GlobalPaneSelection(restoredPanes);

        // First registration wins for a key, so a host that composed two
        // sources for one area gets the one it wrote first rather than a throw
        // on the page's first render.
        _aiSources = AiSources
            .GroupBy(source => source.AreaKey, StringComparer.Ordinal)
            .ToDictionary(group => group.Key, group => group.First(), StringComparer.Ordinal);

        State.Changed += HandleStateChanged;
        State.WrittenElsewhere += HandleTasksWrittenElsewhere;
        InboxState.Changed += HandleStateChanged;
        InboxState.Routed += HandleInboxRouted;
        InboxState.BatchRouted += HandleInboxBatchRouted;
        Features.Changed += HandleFeaturesChanged;
        _gitHubIntegrationWasOn = IsFeatureEnabled(TasksFeatures.GitHubIntegration);

        // Optional, the way Settings resolves it: a host without task sync
        // composes no worker, and a test rendering this page should not have to.
        _taskSync = ResolveTaskSync();
        if (_taskSync is not null) _taskSync.Changed += HandleTaskSyncChanged;

        // Optional on the same terms, and for a second reason: this is the window
        // answering questions from outside, so a host that composed no activator
        // simply is not asked, which is the correct behaviour rather than a gap.
        _surfaceAttachment = Services.GetService<SessionsSurfaceActivator>()?.Attach(DescribeSessionsAsync);

        _work = WorkInProgressReader.From(Services);

        await State.InitializeAsync();
        SynchronizePaneAvailability();
        CheckPullRequestsAfterLoad();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Has the Tasks state read, in the background, the pull requests of Done
    /// entries it does not know to have landed — never read, or last read open and
    /// not re-read for a while — so work that merged shows merged without the sync
    /// button. Called after the list loads and after every reload of it.
    /// <para>
    /// Not awaited: the list is already on screen and must stay usable offline, and
    /// the state keeps its own one-at-a-time guard and stays quiet on failure. The
    /// flag is checked here because only the shell knows the feature switches; the
    /// state checks for a configured repository itself.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private void CheckPullRequestsAfterLoad()
    {
        if (!IsFeatureEnabled(TasksFeatures.GitHubIntegration)) return;

        _ = State.CheckPullRequestsAsync();
    }

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (firstRender)
        {
            _selfReference = DotNetObjectReference.Create(this);
            try
            {
                await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("backlogPaneResizer.initialize", _selfReference);
            }
            catch (Exception)
            {
                // Without the interop the separator still resizes by keyboard.
            }

            try
            {
                await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("backlogTakeoverEscape.register", _selfReference);
            }
            catch (Exception)
            {
                // Without it Escape still closes a surface from inside the surface;
                // only the press after focus fell to <body> goes unheard.
            }
        }

        // The Inbox loads the first time its pane is on screen rather than when
        // this page does, because the pane ships behind a flag and is closed by
        // default — a store read for a pane nobody opened is a store read wasted.
        // After every render rather than on a toggle handler, because there are
        // three ways the pane opens (its own toggle, a restored selection, and
        // the feature switching on) and one place to notice is better than three.
        if (InboxPaneVisible && !_inboxInitialized)
        {
            _inboxInitialized = true;
            _inboxWasVisible = true;
            await InboxState.InitializeAsync();
        }
        else if (InboxPaneVisible != _inboxWasVisible)
        {
            // Every later open runs the resurface sweep again, so a deferred
            // item due since the pane was last closed is back before anyone
            // reads the queue. The flag moves first: the sweep re-renders, and
            // the render after it must not see a second opening.
            _inboxWasVisible = InboxPaneVisible;
            if (InboxPaneVisible) await InboxState.OpenedAsync();
        }

        // The two branches below run on every render, not only the first: a
        // surface can open and close any number of times after it.
        if (WorkspaceVisible)
        {
            _focusSurfacePending = false;

            if (_workspaceWasHidden)
            {
                _workspaceWasHidden = false;

                // A takeover unmounts the pane layout, and the bounds are measured
                // from that element, so the window's resize listener has nothing to
                // report for as long as one is open. Without this, a window resized
                // during a takeover would leave the pane capacity describing the
                // window as it was before — until the next resize happened to
                // correct it. Re-measuring here applies the capacity rule for the
                // window the reader actually came back to.
                try
                {
                    await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("backlogPaneResizer.refresh");
                }
                catch (Exception)
                {
                    // Non-fatal for the same reason initialize is: without the
                    // interop the capacity simply stays where it was.
                }
            }

            return;
        }

        _workspaceWasHidden = true;

        // Moving focus onto the surface is what announces the context change and
        // makes Escape work without a click first. Once per open — guarded on the
        // flag rather than on firstRender, because a re-render inside an open
        // surface must not drag focus back out of whatever it is now on.
        if (!_focusSurfacePending) return;

        _focusSurfacePending = false;
        try
        {
            await _surfaceElement.FocusAsync();
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // Focus is an improvement on where the browser would have left it,
            // not something the surface depends on.
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Whether the tools surface is on screen. Unlike the three global
    /// panes this is a plain disclosure with no capacity rule — it takes the whole
    /// screen instead of a place in the side stack, so nothing else renders beside
    /// it — but it is still gated on the feature, so turning the feature off
    /// returns the reader to the workspace.</summary>
    private bool ToolsVisible => _surface == WorkspaceSurface.Tools && IsFeatureEnabled(DevPcFeatures.SystemTools);

    /// <summary>Whether the dashboard is on screen. The same shape as
    /// <see cref="ToolsVisible"/>, and mutually exclusive with it by construction
    /// rather than by a rule written here: both read the one surface field.</summary>
    private bool DashboardVisible => _surface == WorkspaceSurface.Dashboard && DashboardOffered;

    private bool DashboardOffered => IsFeatureEnabled(DashboardFeatures.Dashboard);

    /// <summary>Whether the session list is on screen. The same shape again, for
    /// another context: the flag it reads is Sessions', not the shell's, because
    /// the pane is that context's too.</summary>
    private bool SessionsVisible => _surface == WorkspaceSurface.Sessions && SessionsOffered;

    private bool SessionsOffered => IsFeatureEnabled(SessionFeatures.Sessions);

    /// <summary>Whether the pull requests list is on screen. The same shape again,
    /// and the same context as the session list, under a flag of its own.</summary>
    private bool PullRequestsVisible => _surface == WorkspaceSurface.PullRequests && PullRequestsOffered;

    private bool PullRequestsOffered => IsFeatureEnabled(SessionFeatures.PullRequests);

    /// <summary>Whether the workspace — the main view and its side panes — is on
    /// screen. Anything that is not a takeover is the workspace, including a
    /// surface whose feature has been switched off.</summary>
    private bool WorkspaceVisible => !ToolsVisible && !DashboardVisible && !SessionsVisible && !PullRequestsVisible;

    /// <summary>The view the workspace shows: the one chosen, unless its feature
    /// has since been switched off — then Tasks, which is always offered. The
    /// choice itself is kept, so switching the feature back on shows it again.</summary>
    private ShellView EffectiveView => _view switch
    {
        ShellView.Roadmap when !RoadmapViewOffered => ShellView.Tasks,
        ShellView.InProgress when !InProgressViewOffered => ShellView.Tasks,
        _ => _view
    };

    /// <summary>Whether the work in progress group offers In progress: while either
    /// of the lists it summarises is on.</summary>
    private bool InProgressViewOffered => SessionsOffered || PullRequestsOffered;

    /// <summary>Whether the In progress view is on screen.</summary>
    private bool InProgressViewVisible => WorkspaceVisible && EffectiveView == ShellView.InProgress;

    /// <summary>Whether the task list is in front of the reader — the Tasks view, or
    /// the Board or the Calendar laying out the same filtered rows, on screen in
    /// the workspace, not merely chosen underneath a takeover. The GitHub check acts
    /// on the list and is offered on this answer; so is the Tasks area of Ask AI.</summary>
    private bool TasksListOnScreen => WorkspaceVisible && EffectiveView is ShellView.Tasks or ShellView.Board or ShellView.Calendar;

    /// <summary>How the Tasks pane lays out its filtered rows: columns of cards on
    /// the Board view, the month calendar on the Calendar view, the list otherwise.</summary>
    private TasksLayout TasksPaneLayout => EffectiveView switch
    {
        ShellView.Board => TasksLayout.Board,
        ShellView.Calendar => TasksLayout.Calendar,
        _ => TasksLayout.List
    };

    /// <summary>What the Board's columns are, as the shell remembered them beside
    /// the view. A name it no longer knows reads as Status.</summary>
    private TaskBoardGrouping BoardGrouping =>
        Enum.TryParse<TaskBoardGrouping>(ShellNavigation.BoardColumns, out var grouping)
        && Enum.IsDefined(grouping)
        && char.IsLetter(ShellNavigation.BoardColumns[0])
            ? grouping
            : TaskBoardGrouping.Status;

    private void OnBoardGroupingChanged(TaskBoardGrouping grouping) =>
        ShellNavigation.SetBoardColumns(grouping.ToString());

    /// <summary>Whether what is on screen can show several repositories at once:
    /// the task list, the Dashboard, the session list, or the pull requests list.
    /// Devbook reads one, and Tools reads none, so on those a chip is a single
    /// select and the scope is kept to its anchor.</summary>
    private bool RepositoryScopeTakesSeveral =>
        TasksListOnScreen || InProgressViewVisible || DashboardVisible || SessionsVisible || PullRequestsVisible;

    /// <summary>Offered while the feature is on and there is an area to ask about.
    /// It used to be the task list alone; every area answers now, so the button
    /// follows whichever of them is on screen.</summary>
    private bool AiAssistantOffered => IsFeatureEnabled(AppFeatures.AiAssistant) && OpenAiAreas.Count > 0;

    /// <summary>
    /// The areas on screen that have a source behind them, in a fixed order.
    /// <para>
    /// The order is the shell's, not the reader's: the chips would otherwise
    /// reorder themselves as panes opened and closed, and a row that shuffles
    /// under the pointer is a row nobody can aim at. It reads the way the
    /// workspace does — the panes left to right, then the takeovers — and only the keys a host actually composed a source for
    /// count, so a host without a Devbook source never offers a Devbook chip.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<IAiContentSource> OpenAiAreas
    {
        get
        {
            var keys = new List<string>(7);
            if (WorkspaceVisible && InboxPaneVisible) keys.Add("inbox");
            if (TasksListOnScreen) keys.Add("tasks");
            if (RoadmapViewVisible) keys.Add("roadmap");
            if (WorkspaceVisible && DevbookPaneVisible) keys.Add("devbook");
            if (DashboardVisible) keys.Add("dashboard");
            if (SessionsVisible) keys.Add("sessions");
            if (ToolsVisible) keys.Add("tools");

            return [.. keys.Where(_aiSources.ContainsKey).Select(key => _aiSources[key])];
        }
    }

    /// <summary>The area a question goes to: the chosen one while it is still on
    /// screen, else the first open one, else nothing — in which case the panel
    /// is not offered at all.</summary>
    private string? EffectiveAiScope
    {
        get
        {
            var open = OpenAiAreas;
            if (open.Count == 0) return null;

            return _aiScope is not null && open.Any(area => string.Equals(area.AreaKey, _aiScope, StringComparison.Ordinal))
                ? _aiScope
                : open[0].AreaKey;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>The sentence under the panel's title. It names the area when
    /// there is one, because with one area there are no chips to name it.</summary>
    private string AiPanelBody
    {
        get
        {
            var open = OpenAiAreas;
            if (open.Count == 1) return $"Answers from the {open[0].AreaTitle} content.";

            return "Answers from the content of the area chosen above.";
        }
    }

    /// <summary>A press on a scope chip. A choice, held until the next area
    /// opens — see <see cref="_aiScope"/>.</summary>
    private void ChooseAiScope(string areaKey) => _aiScope = areaKey;

    /// <summary>The reader opened an area, so it becomes the default scope. Called
    /// from every gesture that opens one: the pane presses and the surface switch.
    /// Only opens count — closing a pane
    /// says nothing about what the reader wants to ask about, and the fallback in
    /// <see cref="EffectiveAiScope"/> handles a scope that has since gone.</summary>
    private void RememberAiScope(string areaKey) => _aiScope = areaKey;

    private static string AiScopeFor(GlobalPane pane) => pane switch
    {
        GlobalPane.Inbox => "inbox",
        GlobalPane.Devbook => "devbook",
        // Exhaustive on purpose: a pane added without an area is a pane whose
        // press would silently point Ask AI at the wrong content, and a throw
        // here is found by the first test that presses it.
        _ => throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(pane), pane, "No Ask AI area is defined for this pane.")
    };

    private static string AiScopeFor(ShellView view) => view switch
    {
        ShellView.Tasks => "tasks",
        ShellView.Board => "tasks",
        ShellView.Calendar => "tasks",
        ShellView.Roadmap => "roadmap",
        // No Ask AI source answers for it yet, so the panel falls back to the
        // first open area rather than pointing at this one.
        ShellView.InProgress => "in-progress",
        _ => throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(view), view, "No Ask AI area is defined for this view.")
    };

    /// <summary>Whether the loose takeover group — the Dashboard and Tools — is
    /// offered at all: with both features off it would be an empty group. Sessions
    /// and Pull requests have a group of their own. The name is older than that and
    /// is kept; nothing persists it.</summary>
    private bool SurfaceSwitcherVisible => IsFeatureEnabled(DevPcFeatures.SystemTools) || DashboardOffered;

    /// <summary>Whether the view switch offers the Roadmap. The feature decides
    /// whether there is an option; the option decides what is on screen.</summary>
    private bool RoadmapViewOffered => IsFeatureEnabled(RoadmapFeatures.Roadmap);

    /// <summary>Whether the roadmap is on screen: the Roadmap view, in the
    /// workspace, with its feature on.</summary>
    private bool RoadmapViewVisible => WorkspaceVisible && EffectiveView == ShellView.Roadmap;

    /// <summary>Whether a view's option reads pressed: it is the view on screen.
    /// During a takeover no view is on screen, so none reads pressed.</summary>
    private bool ViewOptionPressed(ShellView view) => WorkspaceVisible && EffectiveView == view;

    /// <summary>
    /// A press on a view's option: that view becomes the workspace's main view, and
    /// a takeover that was up closes, so the press is also the way back.
    /// <para>
    /// The option already pressed changes nothing — one view is always showing, so
    /// there is nothing to switch off to. The Inbox and the Devbook stay as they
    /// were: they are panes beside the view, whichever it is.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private void ShowView(ShellView view)
    {
        if (!WorkspaceVisible) CloseSurface();

        SetView(view);
    }

    /// <summary>Makes <paramref name="view"/> the main view, remembers it for the
    /// next fresh shell instance by its enum name, and points Ask AI at it.</summary>
    private void SetView(ShellView view)
    {
        _view = view;
        ShellNavigation.SetLastView(view.ToString());

        // A view with no Ask AI source leaves the reader's chosen area alone.
        if (_aiSources.ContainsKey(AiScopeFor(view))) RememberAiScope(AiScopeFor(view));
        KeepRepositoryScopeSingleWhereOnlyOneFits();
    }

    /// <summary>Opens or closes the panel. Closing it ends an ask in flight: the
    /// answer would land in a panel nobody is looking at, and the quota it costs
    /// is better kept for a question the reader waits for.</summary>
    private void ToggleAiPanel()
    {
        _aiExpanded = !_aiExpanded;
        if (!_aiExpanded) CancelAsk();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Say whether this window offers the Sessions pane — the shell's half of
    /// <c>open_dashboard</c>, registered with <see cref="SessionsSurfaceActivator"/>
    /// while this window is alive.
    /// <para>
    /// It answers and never navigates. Every agent run calls <c>open_dashboard</c> when
    /// it starts, so a window that moved to the Sessions pane here would move whenever
    /// an agent did something, pulling the reader off whatever they were in the middle
    /// of. Navigating is the reader's act alone: the takeover groups in the header.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Inside <c>InvokeAsync</c> because the request arrives on a request thread with no
    /// relationship to this renderer, and the feature is read there so the answer
    /// describes the window as it is at the moment it is asked.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task<DeliverySurfaceActivation> DescribeSessionsAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        var answer = DeliverySurfaceActivation.Unattached;

        await InvokeAsync(() =>
        {
            cancellationToken.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();

            // Switched off is a real answer, not a failure: the area's key gates the
            // tab, so there is no pane for the reader to open.
            answer = SessionsOffered
                ? DeliverySurfaceActivation.Available
                : DeliverySurfaceActivation.Disabled;
        });

        return answer;
    }

    private void ToggleTools() => ToggleSurface(WorkspaceSurface.Tools);

    private void ToggleDashboard() => ToggleSurface(WorkspaceSurface.Dashboard);

    private void ToggleSessions() => ToggleSurface(WorkspaceSurface.Sessions);

    private void TogglePullRequests() => ToggleSurface(WorkspaceSurface.PullRequests);

    /// <summary>Opens a surface, or returns to the workspace if it was already the
    /// one on screen. Assigning rather than flipping a flag is what makes the
    /// takeovers exclusive: opening one is the same act as closing the other.</summary>
    private void ToggleSurface(WorkspaceSurface surface)
    {
        _focusSessionId = null;
        _focusPullRequest = null;
        _surface = _surface == surface ? WorkspaceSurface.Workspace : surface;
        _focusSurfacePending = _surface != WorkspaceSurface.Workspace;
        RememberSurface();
        KeepRepositoryScopeSingleWhereOnlyOneFits();

        // Opening a takeover opens its area. Returning to the workspace is not an
        // open, and the scope the reader had there before is what the fallback
        // returns to.
        switch (_surface)
        {
            case WorkspaceSurface.Tools:
                RememberAiScope("tools");
                break;
            case WorkspaceSurface.Dashboard:
                RememberAiScope("dashboard");
                break;
            case WorkspaceSurface.Sessions:
                RememberAiScope("sessions");
                break;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Back to the workspace. The view and the side panes were never
    /// touched, so there is nothing to restore — they are simply visible again.</summary>
    private void CloseSurface()
    {
        _focusSessionId = null;
        _focusPullRequest = null;
        _surface = WorkspaceSurface.Workspace;
        RememberSurface();
        KeepRepositoryScopeSingleWhereOnlyOneFits();
    }

    /// <summary>What the reader is looking at, written for the next fresh shell
    /// instance by its enum name.</summary>
    private void RememberSurface() => ShellNavigation.SetLastSurface(_surface.ToString());

    /// <summary>The session a task asked the sessions list to open on, until the
    /// reader leaves the list. Cleared on every other way off it, so opening the
    /// list from the header later is an ordinary opening rather than a replay.</summary>
    private string? _focusSessionId;

    /// <summary>
    /// An AI session a Backlog entry names, opened from the entry: the sessions list
    /// on screen with that session's row marked and focused.
    /// <para>
    /// The mirror of <see cref="OpenPlanEntryAsync"/>, and here for the same reason:
    /// the entry belongs to Tasks and the session to Sessions, and the shell is the
    /// one place that holds both. A list switched off is said out loud rather than
    /// forced open over a setting the reader chose.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// It navigates, which <see cref="DescribeSessionsAsync"/> must never do: this is
    /// the reader's own click, where that is an agent asking from outside.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private Task OpenSessionAsync(string sessionId)
    {
        if (!SessionsOffered)
        {
            Toasts.Publish(ToastMessage.Warning(
                "The sessions list is switched off, so this session cannot be opened here.",
                SessionsOffTestId));

            return Task.CompletedTask;
        }

        // Opened rather than toggled: asked from the list itself, a toggle would close it.
        if (_surface != WorkspaceSurface.Sessions) ToggleSessions();

        // After the switch, which clears the focus on every other way onto the list.
        _focusSessionId = sessionId;

        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    private const string SessionsOffTestId = "tasks-session-sessions-off";

